EIM CONFIG ADMITTED TO THE ROOT ALLOW LIST
==========================================
Time : 2026-10-14 22:10

DECISION (user)
  Add eim_config.toml to the workspace root allow list. Leave the non-ASCII file names under workspace\
  alone for now.

WHY IT IS LEGITIMATE
  eim_config.toml is written by the ESP-IDF installer (EIM) and the tool expects it in the project root
  - it is tool-generated configuration, not agent output, which is why the self test reported it as a
  WARN (placement) rather than a FAIL (a real rule breach).

CHANGED, IN THE ORDER THE CHANGE PROCESS PRESCRIBES
  1. policy\storage-policy.json -> v1.5.0, updatedAt 2026-10-14
       rootAllowList now 12 entries (added eim_config.toml)
       new rootAllowListNotes records WHY each such file is admitted, to spare the next reader the doubt
  2. policy\output-storage-policy.md section 4 - names the file, explains the reason, and states the
     general rule for future cases: any further tool-generated root file must be registered in
     rootAllowList with its justification
  3. AGENTS.md rule 2 - same, in the injected summary
  4. tools\Test-StoragePolicy.ps1 - fallback allow list updated too (it is only used when a workspace has
     no policy file, but keeping it in sync avoids a confusing divergence), written with a BOM
  5. policy\deploy\global-agents.md rule 3 - generalised wording only ("tool-generated configuration
     files, registered in that workspace's rootAllowList"). Deliberately NOT naming eim_config.toml at
     the global layer: that layer applies to every workspace and should carry the principle, not one
     machine's tooling detail.
  6. Global copy redeployed; source and %USERPROFILE%\.dsh\AGENTS.md verified byte-identical
  7. Rule backup refreshed

RESULT
  Self test now reports FAIL=0 WARN=1. The single remaining warning is the non-ASCII file names under
  workspace\ (wind-sensor material and similar) - user-owned files from another conversation, left
  untouched by decision. Placement is the only issue; reference_file\ would exempt them from the ASCII
  naming rule if they ever need to move.